WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:13.600
Hi, everyone. I'm Dr. Michael Manino. There was a moment a few years back when I was leading

00:13.600 --> 00:19.920
a high stakes cross-disciplinary project under a tight deadline with my team. And the stakes

00:19.920 --> 00:23.680
weren't just about the outcome. They were about proving that our team could perform

00:23.680 --> 00:28.000
under pressure. This would be a great example. We'd be setting a great precedent for our

00:28.000 --> 00:33.920
team. We had talented people, but we weren't moving as one. We were working hard in parallel,

00:33.920 --> 00:38.960
but we weren't truly connected. And under pressure, that misalignment starts to compound.

00:38.960 --> 00:46.240
Communication gets clipped, trust and psychological safety gets tested, decisions take longer. We

00:46.240 --> 00:52.000
needed to get in sync, literally. Teams on the same wavelength literally performed better.

00:52.880 --> 00:58.960
And a study out of Dartmouth actually showed this. Researchers had teams watch silent,

00:58.960 --> 01:05.120
confusing film clips and then discuss what they thought was happening. The most successful teams,

01:05.120 --> 01:10.880
the ones whose brains literally synchronized during the discussions, had one thing in common,

01:10.880 --> 01:16.240
a unique team member who fostered alignment. They weren't the loudest voiced in the room,

01:16.320 --> 01:21.920
but they asked open-ended questions, reflected ideas back, adapted their tone to match the

01:21.920 --> 01:26.560
groups, and they created a safe space for everybody to contribute. That communication

01:26.560 --> 01:32.720
style created deep synchrony, shared understanding and group cohesion. Now, on my own team,

01:32.720 --> 01:38.480
I shifted from pushing for answers to trying to create that space with that communication style.

01:38.480 --> 01:43.680
And almost immediately, I saw a shift in people's minds. We realigned on our mission,

01:43.680 --> 01:49.120
clarified priorities, and agreed not just on what we were doing, but how we'd move together.

01:49.120 --> 01:53.760
That moment unlocked alignment. People started anticipating each other's needs,

01:53.760 --> 01:57.920
recovering from mistakes instantly, and making faster and better decisions.

01:58.880 --> 02:04.480
It felt like team flow. That's an optimal state of consciousness, a collective state of mind where

02:04.480 --> 02:09.520
the whole is greater than the sum of the parts and everything just clicks as if the team operates as

02:09.520 --> 02:15.120
one unified organism with a collective ambition. Everybody's operating at their edge, but in sync.

02:16.080 --> 02:22.640
Now, alignment isn't just a one-time event. It's a living, breathing process. Under pressure,

02:22.640 --> 02:28.080
it can fray fast, and agility is what keeps it alive. Alignment gives you direction,

02:28.080 --> 02:33.200
and agility lets you adapt without losing it. And when you have both, you create the conditions for

02:33.200 --> 02:39.840
team flow. The state where trust, communication, and performance all reinforce one another.

02:40.720 --> 02:46.400
Now, over the next 90 days, you'll face moments where pressure spikes. That's your cue to pause

02:46.400 --> 02:51.520
and check. Are we still truly aligned? And can we pivot without losing that connection?

02:52.400 --> 02:57.040
So here's my challenge to you. This week, take 20 minutes with your team and ask two questions.

02:57.200 --> 03:03.360
What are we really here to accomplish? And how will we move together when things change? How

03:03.360 --> 03:08.960
will we get in sync? Answer honestly, and you'll be building the foundation for alignment, agility,

03:08.960 --> 03:11.440
and team flow. Good luck.

